# Finance 6: The Pricing Field

The earlier chapters used a `module::Field` to price, always with one model and one engine at a time. This chapter treats the object itself: a `module::Field` bundles an instrument, a market, a model, the pricing controls and the simulated paths, so that one object prices, differentiates, simulates and calibrates. After this chapter you can build a `Field` for any of the six models, price a whole strip of options with any engine the model supports, read Greeks and know how each was obtained, move parameters in and out as a vector, define the loss and the objective for a fit, calibrate Heston and SABR to quotes, and use simulated paths to price path-dependent payoffs.

Every program is complete and its output is the output of that program.

## The mental model

- **A `Field` is data.** Its five members (`instrument`, `market`, `model`, `controls`, `paths`) are plain structs of `Series`; change any one and price again.
- **The model is a value.** A `module::Model` holds one of `BlackScholes`, `Sabr`, `Heston`, `Bates`, `RoughHeston`, `RoughBergomi`, exposes its parameters as a flat vector, and can be replaced or priced as a candidate without touching the `Field`.
- **The engine is a choice, not a consequence.** `Analytic`, `Fft`, `Cos`, `MonteCarlo` and `Lattice` price the models they support; a `Field` never switches silently and reports the engines a model supports when you ask for another.
- **A fit is a loss plus a minimiser.** The `Field` prices every quote in one call and reduces the residuals to a number; any minimiser can use that as its objective.

| Entry point | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `Field::price`, `Field::validate` | price the held model, or a candidate, by the chosen engine |
| `Field::residuals`, `loss`, `loss_value`, `objective`, `bind_objective`, `bind_objective_typed` | the quantities a fit needs |
| `Field::simulate`, `simulate_paths`, `clear_paths` | generate and keep the paths of the Monte Carlo engine |
| `Model::parameters`, `holds`, `as`, `visit`, `name` | read, write and identify the held model |
| `pricer::Analytic`, `Fft`, `COS`, `MonteCarlo`, `Lattice` | the engines, usable without a `Field` |
| `instrument::american`, `instrument::bermudan` | instruments with early exercise (page 7) |
| `instrument::make`, `market::make`, `model::make` | build the members from plain numbers |

